// An unsuffixed integer literal is `int32` when NOTHING CONSTRAINS IT, and this one is not constrained by
// anything: a comparison in an `if` has no declared destination to take a type from. It does not fit
// int32, so there is no type to give it.
//
// This fixture used to hold `int64 a = 4294967295;`, which was a parse error because the parser answered
// a question that belongs to the destination. Milestone 5b-B moved the answer to where the destination is
// known, so that spelling now COMPILES and is pinned in tests/int_literal_wide.kama. What survives here
// is the case the parse error was really protecting: a literal typed by its own MAGNITUDE, which would let
// editing a constant silently retype the expression around it.
fn int32 main() {
    if (4294967295 > 3) { return 1; }   // needs `4294967295i64`, or a destination
    return 0;
}
